select 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000002', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: how much Italy do you already know?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"By tradition, the city of Rome was founded in which year?","options":["753 BC","476 AD","1492 AD","27 BC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Roman tradition dates the founding to 753 BC; 476 AD is instead the fall of the Western Roman Empire."},{"prompt":"The Renaissance is usually said to have begun in which Italian city?","options":["Venice","Florence","Naples","Turin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Florence, under families such as the Medici, is widely regarded as the cradle of the Renaissance."},{"prompt":"Dante Alighieri''s most famous poem is known in English as…","options":["The Decameron","The Betrothed","The Divine Comedy","The Canzoniere"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Divine Comedy (La Divina Commedia) follows a journey through Hell, Purgatory, and Paradise."},{"prompt":"Which banking family were the great patrons of art in Renaissance Florence?","options":["The Borgia","The Medici","The Sforza","The Este"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Medici funded artists and thinkers and later produced popes and queens."},{"prompt":"Which maritime republic is famous for its lagoon, canals, and the Doge?","options":["Genoa","Pisa","Amalfi","Venice"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Venice, led by an elected Doge, was a dominant sea power; Genoa, Pisa, and Amalfi were the other great maritime republics."},{"prompt":"Which composer wrote the operas Rigoletto, La Traviata, and Aida?","options":["Giuseppe Verdi","Giacomo Puccini","Antonio Vivaldi","Claudio Monteverdi"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Giuseppe Verdi is the composer; Puccini wrote later works such as Tosca and La Boheme."},{"prompt":"The 19th-century movement that unified the Italian states into one kingdom is called the…","options":["Renaissance","Risorgimento","Reconquista","Reformation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Risorgimento led to the Kingdom of Italy, proclaimed in 1861."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The land: geography and the twenty regions', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Picture Italy''s shape, name its major physical features, and explain why geography split the peninsula into many strong local cultures. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: which three features would you expect along a long, narrow, mountainous peninsula surrounded by sea? By the end you should be able to sketch the boot, place the Alps and the Apennines, and say why travel between regions was historically slow and why regional identity stayed strong."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Italy is a boot-shaped peninsula reaching south into the Mediterranean Sea, with the Alps forming a high wall across the north and the Apennine mountains running like a spine down the length of the country. The fertile Po Valley lies in the north between the Alps and the Apennines. Two large islands, Sicily and Sardinia, complete the national territory. Today the country is divided into twenty regions, among them Lombardy, Tuscany, Lazio, Campania, and Sicily, each with its own capital, dialect traditions, and cooking. Because mountains and seas separated communities, Italy for most of its history was not a single state but a patchwork of cities and small territories. That fragmentation is the key to its culture: strong local loyalties, called campanilismo after the town bell tower, produced dozens of distinct artistic and culinary traditions rather than one."},{"heading":"Key places and figures","text":"Rome, the capital, sits in the central region of Lazio on the Tiber River. Milan anchors the industrial and financial north in Lombardy. Florence is the Tuscan capital on the Arno. Venice rises from a lagoon in the northeast. Naples commands the southern Bay of Naples beneath Mount Vesuvius, the volcano that buried Pompeii in 79 AD. Mount Etna on Sicily is one of the most active volcanoes in the world. The tiny independent states of Vatican City, the seat of the Catholic Church, and San Marino lie entirely within Italian borders. These settings recur throughout the course: Florence for the Renaissance, Venice for sea trade and opera, Rome for the ancient empire and the Church, and Naples for pizza and song."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What mountain range runs down the middle of the peninsula? 2) Name the fertile northern plain. 3) Which two large islands belong to Italy? 4) How many regions does modern Italy have? 5) Which volcano destroyed Pompeii, and in what year? Answers: 1) the Apennines. 2) the Po Valley (Po Plain). 3) Sicily and Sardinia. 4) twenty. 5) Mount Vesuvius, in 79 AD. If you missed the last one, note that Vesuvius still looms over the Bay of Naples today and is closely monitored."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: confusing the Alps (the northern border range) with the Apennines (the central spine). Mistake: treating a region and its capital city as the same thing; Tuscany is the region, Florence the city. Mistake: assuming Italy was always one nation, when unity came only in 1861. Exit: (a) Which range forms Italy''s northern border? (b) Why did so many separate cultures develop on one peninsula? Answers: the Alps; because mountains and seas isolated communities, so strong local identities and traditions grew in each city and region."}]}'::jsonb, 'Italy is one country but twenty administrative regions; a region such as Tuscany is not the same thing as a city such as Florence, its capital.', 12),
  (3, 'The Roman heritage: republic, empire, and law', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Outline the arc of ancient Rome from a small city to an empire, and name the lasting gifts Rome gave to later Europe. Warm-up: the diagnostic placed Rome''s founding at 753 BC; what kinds of things do you think a civilization lasting more than a thousand years would leave behind? By the end you can distinguish the Republic from the Empire, point to Roman engineering and law, and explain how Latin survived in the Italian language."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"According to tradition Rome was founded in 753 BC. For centuries it was a republic, governed by elected officials and a Senate, before Augustus became the first emperor in 27 BC and began the Roman Empire. At its height the empire ringed the whole Mediterranean, which Romans called mare nostrum, our sea. Rome spread a shared language, Latin, a system of written law, a network of paved roads, and engineering such as aqueducts that carried water for many miles. The Western Roman Empire fell in 476 AD, but its institutions, especially law and the Latin language, outlived it. Modern Italian, along with Spanish, French, Portuguese, and Romanian, descends directly from the Latin that Roman soldiers, traders, and settlers once spoke across the empire."},{"heading":"Key works and figures","text":"Rome''s monuments still define the city. The Colosseum, a vast amphitheatre completed around 80 AD, held tens of thousands of spectators for games. The Pantheon, a temple to all the gods, carries one of the largest unreinforced concrete domes ever built. The Roman Forum was the civic heart of the city. Julius Caesar extended Roman power into Gaul before his assassination in 44 BC; his heir Augustus turned the republic into an empire. Roman writers shaped later literature: Virgil composed the epic Aeneid, Cicero perfected Latin prose and oratory, and Ovid wrote the Metamorphoses. Roman law, later gathered under the emperor Justinian, became the foundation of legal systems across continental Europe."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which came first, the Roman Republic or the Roman Empire? 2) Who was the first Roman emperor? 3) Name two pieces of Roman engineering still visible in Rome. 4) From which ancient language does Italian descend? 5) In what year did the Western Roman Empire fall? Answers: 1) the Republic came first, then the Empire. 2) Augustus. 3) the Colosseum and the Pantheon (aqueducts and the Forum are also fine). 4) Latin. 5) 476 AD. If you mixed up the Republic and Empire, remember Augustus marks the turning point in 27 BC."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: saying Rome was always ruled by emperors; it was a republic for centuries first. Mistake: thinking Latin simply died out, when it evolved into Italian and the other Romance languages. Mistake: dating the fall of Rome to the founding year; 753 BC is the founding, 476 AD the fall of the West. Exit: (a) What did Augustus found in 27 BC? (b) Name one lasting Roman contribution to later Europe. Answers: the Roman Empire; written law, the Latin language, roads, or engineering such as aqueducts and domed concrete are all correct."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep the order straight: first the Roman Republic, then the Roman Empire that followed it; Latin is the ancestor of modern Italian and the other Romance languages.', 14),
  (4, 'Communes and the maritime republics', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how medieval Italian towns governed themselves, name the four great maritime republics, and describe how sea trade set the stage for the Renaissance. Warm-up: if your home city ran its own government, raised its own army, and traded across the sea, how might that change its wealth and confidence? By the end you can define a commune, list Venice, Genoa, Pisa, and Amalfi, and connect their trading wealth to the art that followed."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"After the fall of Rome, Italy had no single ruler. During the Middle Ages many northern and central towns organized themselves as communes, self-governing cities run by their own citizens and councils rather than by a distant king. Some coastal communes grew enormously wealthy through sea trade and became known as the maritime republics: Venice, Genoa, Pisa, and Amalfi. They built fleets, opened trading posts across the Mediterranean, and carried goods, ideas, and money between Europe, the Byzantine world, and the East. Rivalries were fierce, both between cities and within them; the long struggle between factions loyal to the pope, the Guelphs, and those loyal to the Holy Roman Emperor, the Ghibellines, ran through this whole period. The commercial wealth these cities gathered would soon pay for the Renaissance."},{"heading":"Key works and figures","text":"Venice, led by an elected ruler called the Doge, dominated trade with the eastern Mediterranean and built the basilica of St Mark''s, rich with gold mosaics. Genoa was Venice''s great rival on the sea and the home city of the navigator Christopher Columbus, born there around 1451. Pisa left the famous leaning bell tower beside its cathedral, begun in the 12th century, which tilts because of soft ground. Amalfi, smallest of the four, was an early power credited with advances in seafaring. The Venetian merchant Marco Polo traveled overland to the court of Kublai Khan in China in the 13th century and dictated an account of his journeys that fascinated Europe for generations."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What was a commune? 2) Name the four maritime republics. 3) What title did the elected leader of Venice hold? 4) Which maritime republic was the birthplace of Christopher Columbus? 5) Who were the Guelphs and the Ghibellines? Answers: 1) a self-governing medieval town run by its own citizens. 2) Venice, Genoa, Pisa, and Amalfi. 3) the Doge. 4) Genoa. 5) rival factions supporting, respectively, the pope and the Holy Roman Emperor. If you forgot one of the four republics, Amalfi is the one most often left out."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming medieval Italy had a king, when it was a patchwork of independent communes and territories. Mistake: listing only Venice and Genoa; Pisa and Amalfi complete the four maritime republics. Mistake: thinking the Guelph and Ghibelline conflict was about religion rather than loyalty to pope or emperor. Exit: (a) How did the maritime republics grow rich? (b) Why did their wealth matter for what came next? Answers: through long-distance sea trade across the Mediterranean; the money and worldliness they gathered funded the artists and scholars of the Renaissance."}]}'::jsonb, 'A commune was a self-governing town; a maritime republic was a coastal commune that grew rich and powerful through sea trade. All four maritime republics were communes, but not every commune was maritime.', 13),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1: land, Rome, and the medieval cities', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The mountain range that forms a wall across the north of Italy is the…","options":["Apennines","Alps","Dolomites only","Pyrenees"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Alps border the north; the Apennines run down the center of the peninsula."},{"prompt":"How many administrative regions does modern Italy have?","options":["12","15","20","26"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Italy is divided into twenty regions, such as Lombardy, Tuscany, and Sicily."},{"prompt":"The first Roman emperor, who turned the republic into an empire in 27 BC, was…","options":["Julius Caesar","Augustus","Nero","Constantine"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Augustus became the first emperor; Caesar never held that title."},{"prompt":"Modern Italian is descended from which ancient language?","options":["Greek","Etruscan","Latin","Gothic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Italian and the other Romance languages all evolved from Latin."},{"prompt":"Which of these was NOT one of the four great maritime republics?","options":["Venice","Genoa","Florence","Pisa"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Florence was a powerful inland commune but not a maritime republic; the four were Venice, Genoa, Pisa, and Amalfi."},{"prompt":"The elected head of the Venetian republic was called the…","options":["Doge","Consul","Podesta","Signore"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Venice was led by an elected Doge."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'The Renaissance, its art and architecture', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what the Renaissance was, why it started in Italy, and name its leading artists and buildings. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: you already know it began in Florence and the Medici paid for it; what might wealthy merchants want their money to buy in a city? By the end you can define humanism, connect trading wealth to patronage, and match Leonardo, Michelangelo, and Raphael to their famous works."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The Renaissance, roughly the 14th through 16th centuries, was a rebirth of interest in the art, learning, and ideals of ancient Greece and Rome. It began in Italy, above all in Florence, because the wealth of trade and banking there could pay for artists, architects, and scholars. The guiding outlook was humanism, which placed new value on human reason, achievement, and the study of classical texts. Patrons, families and individuals who commissioned and funded art, drove the whole system; the Medici of Florence were the most famous. Painters mastered linear perspective to create convincing depth, and sculptors and architects studied the proportions of ancient buildings. The movement spread from Florence to Rome, Venice, and beyond, reshaping how Europe pictured the human figure, the natural world, and the design of cities."},{"heading":"Key works and figures","text":"Leonardo da Vinci, painter, engineer, and scientist, created the Mona Lisa and the wall painting of the Last Supper in Milan. Michelangelo Buonarroti carved the marble David in Florence and painted the vast ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in the Vatican, later adding its Last Judgment. Raphael painted the School of Athens, a fresco gathering the great philosophers of antiquity. Earlier, Giotto had already begun to give painted figures real weight and emotion, and Botticelli painted the Birth of Venus. In architecture, Filippo Brunelleschi engineered the enormous dome of Florence Cathedral, a feat of construction that still crowns the city. Later, the Baroque style brought drama: the painter Caravaggio used stark light and shadow, and Gian Lorenzo Bernini filled Rome with dynamic sculpture and fountains."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does the word Renaissance mean? 2) In which city did it begin? 3) Who painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling and carved the David? 4) Which fresco by Raphael shows the philosophers of antiquity? 5) Who engineered the dome of Florence Cathedral? Answers: 1) rebirth. 2) Florence. 3) Michelangelo. 4) the School of Athens. 5) Filippo Brunelleschi. If you attributed the Mona Lisa to Michelangelo, recall that it is by Leonardo da Vinci."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: crediting the Mona Lisa to Michelangelo; it is Leonardo''s. Mistake: thinking the Renaissance simply copied antiquity, when it used classical models to make something new. Mistake: confusing the Renaissance with the later Baroque; Caravaggio and Bernini belong to the Baroque that followed. Exit: (a) What economic condition in Florence made the Renaissance possible? (b) Name the three artists often grouped as its High Renaissance masters. Answers: great wealth from trade and banking, which funded patronage; Leonardo da Vinci, Michelangelo, and Raphael."}]}'::jsonb, 'Renaissance means rebirth. The movement reached back to ancient Greece and Rome for models, then went beyond them; do not treat it as merely copying the past.', 15),
  (7, 'Literature: Dante, Petrarch, Boccaccio, Manzoni', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Name the founding writers of Italian literature and say what each contributed. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: you matched Dante to the Divine Comedy; why might it matter that he wrote it in everyday Tuscan speech rather than scholarly Latin? By the end you can line up the three crowns, place the Divine Comedy and the Decameron, and explain how Manzoni''s novel helped settle the national language centuries later."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Italian literature was shaped above all by three early writers from Tuscany, often called the three crowns. Dante Alighieri, Petrarch, and Boccaccio all wrote major works in the Tuscan vernacular, the ordinary spoken language, rather than in scholarly Latin. That choice helped turn the Tuscan dialect into the basis of standard Italian. Dante wrote in the early 1300s, Petrarch and Boccaccio shortly after. Their work ran alongside the Renaissance and gave Italy a literature to match its art. Centuries later, in the 1800s, the novelist Alessandro Manzoni revised his great novel into polished Tuscan Italian, a decision that strongly influenced which form of the language the newly unified nation would adopt. Literature, like painting, thus helped knit a divided peninsula into one culture."},{"heading":"Key works and figures","text":"Dante Alighieri wrote the Divine Comedy (La Divina Commedia), an epic journey through Hell, Purgatory, and Paradise divided into three parts, Inferno, Purgatorio, and Paradiso; the poet Virgil guides him through the first two. Francesco Petrarch, often called the father of humanism, perfected the sonnet in his collection of love poems to Laura, the Canzoniere. Giovanni Boccaccio wrote the Decameron, a hundred tales told by young people sheltering from the Black Death outside Florence. Much later, Alessandro Manzoni wrote The Betrothed (I Promessi Sposi), a historical novel set in 17th-century Lombardy and regarded as a landmark of modern Italian prose. Together these works span the beginning and the maturing of the national literature."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Who are the three crowns of Italian literature? 2) What are the three parts of the Divine Comedy? 3) Who wrote the Decameron, and what frames its tales? 4) Which poet is called the father of humanism? 5) Which 19th-century novelist wrote The Betrothed? Answers: 1) Dante, Petrarch, and Boccaccio. 2) Inferno, Purgatorio, and Paradiso. 3) Boccaccio; the tales are told by young people fleeing the Black Death. 4) Petrarch. 5) Alessandro Manzoni. If you listed Manzoni among the three crowns, note that he lived centuries later."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: counting Manzoni among the three crowns; he belongs to the 1800s, not the 1300s. Mistake: assuming these authors wrote in Latin, when their choice of Tuscan vernacular is exactly what made them so important. Mistake: confusing the Decameron (Boccaccio) with the Divine Comedy (Dante). Exit: (a) Why did writing in Tuscan matter for the Italian language? (b) Name the three parts of Dante''s poem. Answers: it helped make the Tuscan dialect the basis of standard Italian; Inferno, Purgatorio, and Paradiso."}]}'::jsonb, 'Dante, Petrarch, and Boccaccio are often called the three crowns of Italian literature. Writing in the Tuscan vernacular rather than Latin helped shape the modern Italian language.', 14),
  (8, 'Music, opera, and cinema', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Trace Italy''s role in music and film, from Baroque composers and the birth of opera to postwar cinema. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: you linked Verdi to his operas; what features would you expect from a national art form built around solo singing voices? By the end you can place the birth of opera, match Vivaldi, Verdi, and Puccini to their work, and describe neorealism and the films of Fellini."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Italy gave Western music some of its deepest foundations, including the very names of its notes and tempos, written in Italian, and the invention of opera. Opera, drama set entirely to music and sung on stage, took shape in Italy around the year 1600, with the composer Claudio Monteverdi among its pioneers. Over the next centuries Italian opera became a national passion, filling grand houses such as La Scala in Milan. In the 20th century Italy transformed cinema as well. After the Second World War, a group of directors created neorealism, filming ordinary people and hardship in real streets rather than on studio sets. From that soil grew a golden age of Italian film admired around the world, and directors whose personal, imaginative style reached far beyond realism."},{"heading":"Key works and figures","text":"Antonio Vivaldi, a Baroque composer from Venice, wrote the famous set of violin concertos known as The Four Seasons. Giuseppe Verdi became the towering figure of 19th-century opera with Rigoletto, La Traviata, Nabucco, and Aida; his chorus Va, pensiero became an anthem during the Risorgimento. Giacomo Puccini followed with beloved operas including La Boheme, Tosca, Madama Butterfly, and Turandot. In cinema, Roberto Rossellini''s Rome, Open City and Vittorio De Sica''s Bicycle Thieves defined neorealism. Federico Fellini then became world famous for imaginative films such as La Strada, La Dolce Vita, and 8 and a half. Italian violin making also set a standard: Antonio Stradivari of Cremona built instruments still prized above almost all others."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Around what year was opera born, and in which country? 2) Who composed The Four Seasons? 3) Name two operas by Puccini. 4) What is neorealism? 5) Which director made La Dolce Vita? Answers: 1) around 1600, in Italy. 2) Antonio Vivaldi. 3) any two of La Boheme, Tosca, Madama Butterfly, and Turandot. 4) a postwar film movement showing ordinary people and real hardship filmed on location. 5) Federico Fellini. If you mixed up Verdi and Puccini, Verdi is the earlier of the two."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: crediting Aida to Puccini; it is Verdi''s. Mistake: thinking opera is modern, when it dates to around 1600. Mistake: treating Fellini as a neorealist; he began near that movement but became known for a far more imaginative, personal style. Exit: (a) Which composer''s Va, pensiero became a patriotic anthem? (b) Name one landmark neorealist film. Answers: Verdi; Rome, Open City or Bicycle Thieves."}]}'::jsonb, 'Opera is sung drama that began in Italy around 1600; neorealism is a film movement that began in Italy around 1945. Both are Italian inventions, but keep the two art forms and their centuries apart.', 14),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2: Renaissance, letters, music, and film', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Who painted the Mona Lisa and the Last Supper?","options":["Michelangelo","Raphael","Leonardo da Vinci","Botticelli"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both are works of Leonardo da Vinci."},{"prompt":"Filippo Brunelleschi is best known for engineering the…","options":["Colosseum","dome of Florence Cathedral","leaning tower of Pisa","St Mark''s in Venice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Brunelleschi designed and built the great dome of Florence Cathedral."},{"prompt":"The three parts of Dante''s Divine Comedy are Inferno, Purgatorio, and…","options":["Paradiso","Decameron","Canzoniere","Aeneid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The third part is Paradiso; the Decameron and Canzoniere are by Boccaccio and Petrarch."},{"prompt":"Which collection of tales did Giovanni Boccaccio write?","options":["The Betrothed","The Decameron","The Four Seasons","The Aeneid"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Boccaccio wrote the Decameron; The Betrothed is Manzoni''s novel."},{"prompt":"The opera La Boheme was composed by…","options":["Giuseppe Verdi","Antonio Vivaldi","Giacomo Puccini","Claudio Monteverdi"],"answer":2,"explanation":"La Boheme is by Giacomo Puccini."},{"prompt":"Neorealism was a movement in Italian…","options":["painting","opera","cinema","architecture"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Neorealism was a postwar film movement using real locations and ordinary people."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Food, festivals, unification, and modern Italy', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe Italy''s regional food and festivals, outline how the country was unified, and name its strengths in design and fashion today. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: you identified the Risorgimento as the unification movement; if a country unites only in 1861, what might still vary from region to region long afterward? By the end you can explain regional cuisine, place the key unification dates, and name modern Italian design icons."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Italian food is famously regional: the north favors butter, rice for risotto, and filled pastas, while the south leans on olive oil, tomatoes, and dried pasta. Pizza was born in Naples, espresso is drunk across the country, and specialties such as Parmigiano-Reggiano cheese and prosciutto ham are tied to particular places. Festivals are equally local and lively, from the masked Carnival of Venice to the Palio horse race in Siena and the Epiphany visit of the gift-bringing figure Befana. Politically, Italy was unified only in the 19th century through the Risorgimento. The Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in 1861, with Rome becoming the capital in 1871. After the Second World War, a 1946 referendum abolished the monarchy and Italy became a republic, later a founding member of the European community."},{"heading":"Key works and figures","text":"The Risorgimento had several architects. Giuseppe Mazzini supplied the ideals of a unified nation. Giuseppe Garibaldi, a daring general, led the volunteer Expedition of the Thousand that won the south in 1860. Camillo Cavour, the shrewd prime minister of Piedmont-Sardinia, guided the diplomacy, and Victor Emmanuel II became the first king of a united Italy in 1861. In modern times Italy leads in design and fashion. Milan is a global fashion capital, home to houses such as Armani, Prada, Versace, Gucci, and Ferragamo. Italian industrial and automotive design is equally celebrated, from Ferrari and Lamborghini sports cars to the Vespa scooter and a long tradition of furniture and product design. These living crafts carry the country''s old eye for beauty into the present."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name one ingredient typical of the north and one of the south. 2) In which city was pizza born? 3) Who led the Expedition of the Thousand? 4) In what year was the Kingdom of Italy proclaimed, and when did it become a republic? 5) Which city is Italy''s fashion capital? Answers: 1) north: butter or rice; south: olive oil or tomatoes. 2) Naples. 3) Giuseppe Garibaldi. 4) proclaimed in 1861; a republic from 1946. 5) Milan. If you reversed the dates, remember the kingdom came first in 1861 and the republic much later in 1946."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining one national cuisine, when Italian cooking is intensely regional. Mistake: swapping the dates, 1861 for the kingdom and 1946 for the republic. Mistake: crediting unification to a single person; Mazzini, Garibaldi, Cavour, and Victor Emmanuel II each played a part. Exit: (a) What did the 1946 referendum decide? (b) Why is Italy still so regionally varied in food and custom? Answers: it abolished the monarchy and made Italy a republic; because the peninsula united only in 1861, so centuries of separate local traditions endure to this day."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep the dates straight: the Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in 1861, and Italy became a republic in 1946. Cuisine and design are living traditions, not fixed historical dates.', 13)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000002' and l.position = v.pos
);
